@page "/normalize"

<PageTitle>Normalize</PageTitle>
<h1>Normalize</h1>
<p>Normalize() is model-owned cleanup that runs before validation. The server round-trip page calls it behind the scenes; this page calls it directly so you can watch the raw values change.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "TrimmedNote.cs", "Normalize.razor", "Normalize.razor.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Normalize()</strong> is model-owned cleanup, not a validation rule — it trims <strong>Title</strong> and collapses runs of internal whitespace to single spaces.</li>
            <li><strong>NotEmpty</strong> treats a value made only of whitespace as empty — that is FluentValidation's own semantics, not something Normalize() causes, so a blank-looking Title already fails presence before Normalize() ever runs.</li>
            <li><strong>Body</strong> is a multiline field (<code>FormidableInputTextArea</code>), trimmed by <strong>Normalize()</strong> the same way.</li>
            <li>Server filters call <code>Normalize()</code> automatically before validating (the server round-trip page relies on that).</li>
            <li>Client code has no automatic hook unless you ask for one — a page calls <code>Normalize()</code> explicitly, and the cleaned value is only what gets judged if that call happens before validation.</li>
            <li>The engine listens for field-change notifications, not the model itself — code that mutates the model tells the engine which fields changed (<code>EditContext.NotifyFieldChanged</code>, one call per changed field), the same wiring a foreign control uses. <em>Normalize now</em> does exactly that, so a message already on screen re-judges the cleaned value immediately.</li>
            <li><strong>NormalizeOnSubmit</strong> is the automatic hook the bullet above says client code lacks: tick the checkbox below and the plain <em>Submit</em> button (unlike <em>Normalize now</em> and <em>Normalize + submit</em>, it never calls <code>Normalize()</code> itself) trims the model before the profile judges it.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Type <code>  spaced   out  title  </code> into Title, spaces and all, and click <em>Normalize now</em> — the raw value below collapses every run to one space.</li>
            <li>Keep padding Title with spaces — once the raw value passes 40 characters, "Title is 40 characters max" appears as you type. <em>Normalize now</em> trims it back under the limit and the message clears at once: the notification re-judged the cleaned value.</li>
            <li>Replace Title with <code>    Meeting notes about the Q3 rollout    </code> — 42 characters raw, 34 trimmed. Click <em>Normalize + submit</em>: trimming runs before validation, the length rule judges the cleaned value, and the submit succeeds.</li>
            <li>Replace Title with spaces only — "Title is required" appears the moment the field is only spaces, before you touch a button, because NotEmpty already reads it as empty. Click <em>Normalize + submit</em> anyway: normalizing turns the value into a literal empty string, and the submit stays blocked by the same message.</li>
            <li>Tick <em>Normalize automatically on submit</em>, replace Title with <code>    Meeting notes about the Q3 rollout    </code> and click plain <em>Submit</em> — the option trims it before the profile runs, with no manual call, and the submit succeeds. The box now shows the trimmed 34-character value, exactly as a manual Normalize would leave it.</li>
            <li>Untick the box, replace Title with that same raw text again, and click <em>Submit</em> — this time the raw 42-character value fails the 40-character rule, since nothing trimmed it first.</li>
        </ol>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<p>
    <label><input type="checkbox" @bind="_options.NormalizeOnSubmit" /> Normalize automatically on submit</label>
</p>

<FormidableForm @ref="_form" Model="_note" Options="_options">
    <div class="summary-slot">
        <FormidableSummary />
    </div>

    <div class="field"><label>Title <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_note.Title" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" /></label>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _note.Title" /></div>
    <div class="field"><label>Body <FormidableInputTextArea @bind-Value="_note.Body" UpdateOn="InputUpdateMode.OnInput" /></label>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _note.Body" /></div>

    <div class="actions">
        <button type="button" @onclick="NormalizeNow">Normalize now</button>
        <button type="button" @onclick="Submit">Submit</button>
        <button type="button" class="primary" @onclick="NormalizeAndSubmit">Normalize + submit</button>
    </div>
</FormidableForm>

<p><small>Raw values — quotes show the whitespace: Title = "@_note.Title" · Body = "@_note.Body"</small></p>
<p role="status">@_status</p>
